# Maintain the Bedrock Agent Build Journal

While working on this ADK-on-Bedrock agent, keep `docs/bedrock-agent-journal.md`
current. It is a **human-readable** record of how the agent is being built.

## When to update

Update the journal in the **same turn** as any of these:

- A **discovery** (something learned about ADK, Bedrock, the AWS SDK, an error, a
  constraint, or how a piece works).
- A **decision** (choosing an approach, library, model, or config).
- A **change in plan** (abandoning or reworking a prior approach).
- A **notable change** to `bedrock_llm.ts`, `agent.ts`, or `.env`.

Do not wait to be asked. If nothing meaningful changed, don't add noise.

## How to update

- Add a dated entry at the **top of the `## Timeline`** section
  (`### YYYY-MM-DD — short title`), with terse bullets: what happened and why.
- Keep the **`Current state`** summary and **`Open questions / TODO`** in sync
  (check off or add items).
- Write for a human reader: plain language, no unexplained jargon, note the
  *reasoning*, not just the *what*.

## Always cite sources

Whenever a discovery, example, or decision came from an external source (docs,
GitHub, blog, npm, Stack Overflow, web search, etc.):

- Include the **full URL** inline in the relevant timeline bullet.
- Also add/keep it in the **`## Sources / references`** section of the journal,
  with a one-line note on *what it backed up*.
- If a claim came from reading local files (e.g. `node_modules` type defs), say
  so explicitly instead of leaving it unsourced.
- Never present a borrowed example as original — always leave a link back.

## Style

- Prefer short bullets over prose. Link sources when a claim came from the web.
- Be honest about uncertainty (e.g. "compiles but not live-tested").
